# Accepted vulnerabilities, with justification and an expiry date.
#
# Trivy re-reports an entry once its expiry passes, so nothing here is suppressed
# permanently — each one comes back for review. Do not add an entry without a reason
# and a date.
#
# Review all entries when bumping keycloak.version.

# java-21-openjdk-headless — bundled LibPNG in the Red Hat OpenJDK build.
# No fixed version exists at time of writing (Trivy status: "affected"). Clears when
# Red Hat ships a patched JDK and Keycloak rebuilds its base image. Keycloak does not
# process PNG data on any request path reachable by an unauthenticated caller.
CVE-2026-22020 exp:2026-10-17

# io.netty:netty-handler 4.1.136.Final — fixed in 4.1.137.Final, which Keycloak 26.7.4
# does not yet bundle. Nothing to do here but wait for the next Keycloak release; this
# is not a dependency this project declares or can override.
CVE-2026-75595 exp:2026-10-17

# com.microsoft.sqlserver:mssql-jdbc 13.2.1 — the advisory's fixed versions
# (10.2.4.jre11, 11.2.4.jre11, ...) are all older than the installed 13.2.1, so this
# looks like a version-comparison artifact rather than real exposure. Moot in any case:
# the image is built with --db=postgres, so the SQL Server driver is never loaded.
CVE-2025-59250 exp:2026-10-17

# --- Added 2026-09-25: newly disclosed, none fixable from this repository ---------------
# These appeared in Trivy's database without any change to the image, and blocked three
# unrelated dependency PRs. Keycloak 26.7.4 (2026-09-16) remains the latest release, so
# there is nothing newer to bump to.

# pcre2 / pcre2-syntax 10.40-6.el9 — Red Hat marks CVE-2026-86145 "will_not_fix" and
# CVE-2026-89161 "affected"; no patched el9 package exists or is planned. Clears only when
# Keycloak moves to a newer UBI base.
CVE-2026-86145 exp:2026-10-17
CVE-2026-89161 exp:2026-10-17

# org.bouncycastle:bcprov-jdk18on 1.84 — fixed in 1.85, but the jar is bundled in the
# Keycloak image (both the admin CLI and, importantly, the server runtime at
# /opt/keycloak/lib/lib/main/). This project cannot override it; only a Keycloak release
# can. REVIEW FIRST at expiry: unlike the other entries here, a fix exists upstream, so
# check for a Keycloak release bundling bcprov >= 1.85 before renewing.
CVE-2026-8763 exp:2026-10-17
CVE-2026-13506 exp:2026-10-17

# org.freemarker:freemarker 2.3.32 — "path traversal via malformed locale identifier",
# fixed in 2.3.35. NOT REACHABLE in Keycloak 26.7.4; verified by reading the 26.7.4
# sources rather than assumed:
#
#   1. Keycloak never passes a Locale to FreeMarker. DefaultFreeMarkerProvider calls
#      cfg.getTemplate(templateName, "UTF-8") — the no-Locale overload — and setLocale()
#      is never called on any FreeMarker Configuration. Localized lookup therefore uses
#      the JVM default locale, fixed at startup. Every caller (login forms, email,
#      welcome page, admin/account console, error handler) uses the same 3-arg
#      processTemplate(Map, String, Theme) signature, which has no Locale parameter.
#      Template names are literals or enum-derived, never user input.
#   2. The resolved locale is allowlisted anyway: DefaultLocaleSelectorProvider.findLocale
#      matches ui_locales / kc_locale / Accept-Language against the realm's configured
#      supported locales, so a malformed identifier never becomes a Locale.
#
# The jar is bundled in the Keycloak image and cannot be overridden from this repository.
# Re-check this analysis if Keycloak changes how themes resolve templates.
CVE-2026-84939 exp:2026-10-17
